# Sales Performance Dashboard

An interactive Power BI dashboard that analyzes retail sales across departments, brands, and time, built on a star-schema data model of 2.6 million transaction lines.



## Project Goal

To give a retail team a quick view of where sales come from and how they change over time. Slicers for date, department, brand, and household income category let users drill into any segment.

## Dashboard Contents

- **KPI cards:** Total Sales, Order Count, Total Units, and Average Order Value
- **Sales by department:** a bar chart comparing departments
- **Sales by brand:** a pie chart of National vs. Private label sales
- **Monthly sales trend:** a column chart of sales by month
- **Slicers:** date, department, brand, and household income category

## Key Insights

- The data covers **8.01M in total sales** from **276,484 orders**, an average order value of **28.98**, across **2,500 households**, **582 stores**, and about **92,000 products** (January 2017 to December 2018).
- **Grocery is the core of the business**, with **50.9%** of sales (4.08M). The next largest departments are Drug & General Merchandise (13.1%), Produce (6.9%), Meat (6.8%), and Gas Kiosk (6.8%).
- **National brands drive most sales:** **72%** (5.77M) against **28%** (2.24M) for private-label products.
- **Monthly sales are stable after a ramp-up.** They grow from 58K in January 2017 to about 350K by May 2017, then stay between roughly 355K and 412K, peaking in **October 2018 (412K)**.
- **Thursday and Wednesday are the busiest days** (1.40M and 1.36M in sales), and Sunday and Monday are the slowest (about 1.0M each).

## Tools Used

- **Power BI**: star-schema data model (FactSales plus DimDate, DimProduct, and DimHousehold), DAX measures, and visuals
